  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1148 AM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nA substantial stream of atmospheric river moisture continues to\nspread across the region this afternoon. Widespread rainfall\npersists below 11000 feet. This has already produced a\nstreamflow response on rivers in southwest CO. Well over an \ninch of rain has fallen across southwest CO in the past 24 hours\nand more is on the way. Flood warnings are in place for the La \nPlata, Mancos, San Juan and Navajo rivers due to the forecasted \nvolume of water that's expected to pass through these basins. \nWidespread rain showers, including some embedded thunderstorms,\nwill bring valley rainfall to near an inch for the central \nvalleys, and 2 to 2.5 inches for the Four Corners valleys \neastward, with less up north. 1.5 to 2.5 inches is likely for \nthe Book Cliffs/Tavaputs and central mountains while the San \nJuans may see 3 to 4 inches of liquid precipitation. The Flood \nWatch remains in effect for southeast Utah and southwest \nColorado accounting for saturated soils and excessive rainfall \nproducing additional localized flooding and debris flows near \nsusceptible washes, arroyos and burn scars.\n\nWinter Weather Conditions:\n\nSnow levels will remain above 10K feet, before dropping late \nthis afternoon onwards. Amounts remain in the 12 to 18 inch \nrange with locally higher amounts possible keeping Winter Storm \nWarnings in effect. The Advisories also remain in place for the \ncentral mountains, Grand Mesa, and Flat Tops where 5 to 10 \ninches of snow are expected. As snow levels lower late Sunday \nnight into Monday morning, some of this snow will reach pass \nlevel. Be prepared for winter driving conditions during the \nMonday morning commute along US-550, US-50, and along I-70 over \nVail Pass, with lighter impacts north on highway 40 west of \nRabbit Ears Pass.\n\nNEXT WEEK:\n\nBehind this system, conditions dry out for a few days as high \namplitude ridging moves overhead. We'll see a slight warm up through \nthe mid-week period, although highs remain 5-10 degrees below \nnormal for early November. Looking further out, a quick moving \nclipper system has the potential to bring some light showers to the \nregion mid-to-late next week, although model agreement is poor.",
